const editorial = {
  heading: "What your current detector is actually built to do",
  html: `
<p>Almost every home in the country has a carbon monoxide alarm. Almost nobody has looked at one closely. It sits there with a green light on, and that green light is the reason you have never thought about it again.</p>

<p>The light indicates the unit has power. It does not indicate the sensor still works. Electrochemical carbon monoxide sensors degrade — manufacturers put the service life at seven to ten years, the International Association of Fire Chiefs says five to ten — and home inspectors routinely pull units off walls that expired years ago and are still glowing green.</p>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/co-ed-basement.jpg" alt="Plug-in carbon monoxide detector mounted near a furnace and water heater, reading 000 PPM" width="1400" height="1045" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>The two appliances most likely to produce carbon monoxide, and the outlet between them.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Homeowners describe the failure in almost identical terms. One, troubleshooting a unit that was chirping while showing nothing: <em>"I look at it, and it has a solid green light."</em> The light was working. The sensor was not.</p>

<p>To be fair about it: alarms built after August 2009 are required to signal end of life, usually a chirp every thirty seconds or an error code on the display. That works while the electronics still have power. The failure that catches people is quieter than that — a sensor that has stopped detecting on a unit that still lights up, because a dead sensor and a working one look identical from across the room.</p>

<h3>The 70 PPM rule, and why it is not a defect</h3>

<p>Carbon monoxide gets called the silent killer because you cannot see it, smell it or taste it. Here is the part almost nobody knows, and it is the reason this comparison exists: your alarm is quiet about it too.</p>

<p>Residential CO alarms in the US are built to UL 2034. Under that standard, an alarm exposed to <strong>70 parts per million must sound somewhere between 60 and 240 minutes</strong>. At 150 PPM it has 10 to 50 minutes. At 400 PPM, 4 to 15 minutes. And <strong>below 30 PPM it is designed not to alarm at all</strong> — most units simply display zero.</p>

<p>Read that first line again. A compliant, correctly functioning, brand-new alarm is permitted to stay silent for up to four hours while a room holds 70 PPM.</p>

<p>That is not a manufacturing failure and it is not a scandal. The window was written deliberately, with input from fire services and gas utilities, to stop alarms shrieking every time someone runs a gas oven or backs a car out of an attached garage. Nuisance alarms get disconnected, and a disconnected alarm protects nobody. The standard is a considered trade-off and the people who wrote it were not being careless.</p>

<p>But it does mean something specific about the device on your wall. The range where people get headaches, feel nauseous, sleep badly and blame it on a cold — the 10 to 30 PPM band that shows up with a dirty burner or a heat exchanger starting to crack — is the range your alarm is built to report as zero.</p>

<p>People who work with this professionally are blunt about what it means. A heating technician, on a trade forum: <em>"It could stay at 69 all day and not alarm."</em></p>

<p><strong>You are not underprotected because you bought a bad alarm. You are underprotected because you bought the standard one.</strong></p>

<h3>What "low level" actually means</h3>

<p>There is an entire product category built around this gap. Low-level monitors — the ones HVAC technicians and fire investigators carry — alarm at around 25 PPM instead of 70, and they run $120 to $250. They are not code-compliant alarms and are not sold as replacements for one. They exist because the people who deal with carbon monoxide professionally want to see the number long before the legal threshold.</p>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/co-ed-bedroom.jpg" alt="Carbon monoxide detector plugged in beside a bed at night, displaying 023 PPM" width="1400" height="1037" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>23 PPM. High enough to cause headaches overnight, low enough that a standard alarm displays zero.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>What that band looks like in a real house, from someone who went looking: <em>"most of our readings when we had them were 6-10ppm due to dirty oven… we likely would never have known."</em></p>

<p>The interesting development is that a plug-in unit with a continuous readout now costs a fraction of that. It is cheaper than a true low-level monitor and it tells you considerably more than a standard alarm. That is the gap our top pick sits in.</p>

<h3>So should you replace what you have?</h3>

<p>Take it off the wall and read the date on the back. If it is more than seven years old, replace it — genuinely, with anything current, the brand matters far less than the date. If it is recent, keep it. It will do what it was built to do.</p>

<p>Then decide the separate question, which is whether you want to see the number. If your house runs gas appliances, has propane on the property, or has an attached garage, you are covering three or four distinct threats with equipment usually built for one. That is the case for the unit at the top of this list.</p>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/co-ed-cutaway.jpg" alt="Cutaway of the CarbonOne Safe showing the sensor board and battery, beside a cross-section of a house showing detector coverage on two floors" width="1600" height="568" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>Guidance is one unit per floor, within about 10 feet of each bedroom door.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>How we scored these five</h3>

<p>Five criteria, fixed before a single product was ranked: live PPM display, low-level sensitivity, rated service life, multi-gas coverage, and battery backup. Every number comes from the manufacturer's own published specification or from the published standard itself, and every score is printed on the card — so you can argue with a specific bar rather than with the conclusion.</p>

<p>Our top pick scores lowest of all five on service life. We printed that too.</p>

<h3>Your furnace will run again tonight</h3>

<p>It ran last night. The alarm on your wall did not tell you what the level was in the hour after it kicked on, because it is not built to, and it will not tell you tomorrow night either. It will sit there showing a green light, the way it has for however many years it has been up there, and you will keep assuming.</p>

<p>That is the whole decision, and it is a smaller one than it looks. Not whether carbon monoxide is dangerous — you know that already or you would not have gone looking. Not whether you need a detector; you almost certainly have one. Just whether you want to keep guessing at what is in the air your family is breathing, through the five months of the year when the windows stay shut and the furnace runs every night.</p>

<p><strong>One of these five shows you the number. The other four wait.</strong></p>
`,
};

